/*****************************************************************************
    THE AWKWARDNESS QUESTION, ANSWERED (Tony, 2026-11-18 asked; the census and its strokes, 2026-11-19)
*****************************************************************************/
    Of the five things that made kant awkward to write (from typeset): THREE WERE ALREADY SPELLED and simply unused --
    control flow (break, continue, && ||, an indented multi-statement else, or as else-if), a parse result by label
    (*t.name, for e in t) and list walking (for e in h, iterate as a kept cursor). Measured on both grammars, in a body.
    SEVERAL ARGUMENTS are a convention, ruled: a request field in (the callee reads argument.w), a record out (return *o);
    f(a, b) is to refuse by name (fixit multiArgLosesBody). HOLDER OR HELD: the compare law is BUILT (a holder against a
    value refuses, "h holds qa; compare *h"); a := as an if / while / do condition refusing with its cure is on branch
    capture-cond for the buy. A rule's result is true when it matched, an empty capture included (ruled).
    Open: typeset's rewrite with these spellings (queued, an afternoon forms stroke); the deep review of the kant code
    (dispatched, waits); [set]* matching empty (measured, not small; reported). The escapes proposal (one decoder) is
    unruled and lives in git (151524fc).
    Evidence: docs/ergoCensus.md (every spelling, both grammars, its row); docs/kantPatterns.md (how to write them);
    fixtures incant/pop/ifElseShapeT and ruleResultT.

/*****************************************************************************
    Description
*****************************************************************************/
Two roads. The old road is incant/grammar with C++ rule actions; action bodies and forms still parse on it. The new road is incant/kantGrammar: each rule's action is kant and builds an instruct, which runs later.
The jitter compiles a rule through the rule door (jitterRule), one rule per stroke; a compiled rule must answer as the interpreted one does on every row.
A regular rule names its operator (op="...") and the shared default action builds it; no control rule keeps a hand-written action (seal 436).
The design and its reasoning are in IncantForms/WorkingOn/macros ("Kant in kant: the plan").

